Supabase Root 2021 CA — the trust anchor for every Supabase Postgres endpoint, including
the Supavisor connection poolers. Supabase signs those certificates with its OWN root, not
a public one, so the relay's bundled Mozilla roots (webpki-roots) do not chain to them and
the handshake fails with UnknownIssuer. NXF_RELAY_PG_CA_FILE is the lever that fixes it —
see crates/server/src/tls_pg.rs and docs/runbooks/managed-postgres-supabase.md.

  subject/issuer : C=US, ST=Delware, L=New Castle, O=Supabase Inc, CN=Supabase Root 2021 CA
  valid          : 2021-04-28 .. 2031-04-26
  SHA-256        : 80:70:25:AD:50:D4:ED:21:9D:2C:9C:7D:29:9C:00:4F:82:4E:B0:0C:F7:F6:5A:FE:F6:07:D0:7B:72:E6:CA:FA

PINNED IN THE REPO ON PURPOSE, not downloaded at job time: a trust anchor that arrives over
the network on every run is a trust anchor nobody reviews. Rotating it is then a visible,
reviewed change. Verify this file against your own project's copy (Supabase dashboard ->
Project Settings -> Database -> SSL Configuration -> Download certificate) with:

  openssl x509 -in <downloaded>.crt -noout -fingerprint -sha256

This is a PUBLIC root certificate, not a secret. Text outside the BEGIN/END block is
ignored by both openssl and rustls-pemfile, which is why this note lives in the file.

